      "writtenOn": "2023-05-16T09:56:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The return value of `self.inner.lock()` is a temporary value in the expression that gets assigned to `ee`. Under normal circumstances, this temporary would be dropped at the end of the expression (that is, before the call to `writer.write`), but due to [temporary lifetime extension](https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/destructors.html#temporary-lifetime-extension), the temporary values in this particular expression are kept alive until the end of the scope instead. (If this didn\u0027t happen, then expressions of the form `let foo \u003d \u0026value_expression` would never be usable since the referent would be dropped before you could use `foo`.)\n\nSo the fix is to, instead of holding a reference to the value inside the lock, make a copy of it, and then write the copy after releasing the lock. That would be your option b (assuming that the type is Copy), and option b does not trigger temporary lifetime extension, so the lock is dropped before the write.",
      "parentUuid": "7b467fe3_bfc363ec",
      "revId": "935d7a0b7ee5fe2a5003ea38f77f454c15207162",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    }
